\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs28\b TOC EXAMINER\b0\fs22\par
\par
\b PURPOSE\b0\par
Scans selected books for mismatches between the book's title/header markers: \\toc1 (long TOC title) against the book's main title, combining \\mt/\\mt1/\\mt2/\\mt3 in the order they appear in the text (some books split the title across more than one \\mt marker); and \\toc2 (short TOC title) against \\h (running header). A literal // used as a soft line break in \\toc1, \\mt, or a BookNames.xml long/short name is ignored (any surrounding space collapses to one space) before comparing. Optionally also compares \\toc1 and \\toc2 against the project's BookNames.xml long/short names for that book. Any missing marker is reported.\par
\par
Results appear in two places:\par
- The results list shows one row per problem found (a mismatch, or a missing marker) - a clean book with no problems produces no rows at all. Clicking a row jumps to the relevant marker in the text.\par
- A TocExaminerReport.html report, listing every problem found with the book, the conflicting marker texts, and two extra columns marking whether the difference is only a matter of letter case (Capitalization only?) or a small, typo/accent-level difference (Spelling?) as opposed to a substantively different phrase, is written to the project folder and opened automatically in your browser after the check runs.\par
\par
\b NUMBERED BOOKS (1, 2, 3)\b0\par
The report also has a section for the numbered books among those selected (1 Samuel to 2 Chronicles, 1 Corinthians to 3 John). For each field - \\h, \\toc1, \\toc2, \\toc3, \\mt, and the BookNames.xml long, short and abbreviation names - it looks at how the number is written in each of those books: a digit and space (1 John), a digit with an ending (1st, 1.\'ba, 1\'aa), a digit with no space (1John), a digit and a period (1. John), a digit and a non-breaking space, a Roman numeral (I John), a number after the name (John 1), or a word (First John). The style most of the books use is taken as usual for that field, and every book that writes it another way is listed, with the text and its style. A table of all numbered books follows, with those cells highlighted. If no style is clearly the most common, every book in that field is highlighted.\par
Each field is compared on its own, so \\toc1 may use a word while \\toc2 uses a digit. Two ordinal words are not compared with each other: First and Primera count as the same style. These findings appear only in the HTML report, not in the results list. The CheckNumberedBooks option turns the section off.\par
\par
If anything unexpected happens, a TocExaminerDebug.log file will appear in the project folder with the details - please share its contents so the check can be fixed.\par
\par
\b HOW TO USE\b0\par
1. Install the Phils Custom (cms) Checks plugin with Install_Phils_tools.exe. Everything comes with the plugin: nothing has to be copied into the cms folder. References in the HTML report can be clicked to show that place in Paratext. The first click makes the browser ask whether to open ParatextGoTo, and Paratext follows only when the text window's scroll group follows other programs.\par
2. In Paratext, choose Tools > Phils Custom (cms) Checks..., select this check and click "Run...". In Paratext's check window, choose the books and set the options (Options...) before you click OK.\par
3. Review the results list for each problem found, and the TocExaminerReport.html report (opened automatically) for the same information in one table.\par
\par
\b OPTIONS\b0\par
\b CompareToc1ToMt\b0\par
\tab Default:  Yes\par
\tab Yes (default) compares \\toc1 against the book's main title, combining \\mt/\\mt1/\\mt2/\\mt3 in the order they appear in the text (some books split the title across more than one \\mt marker). Reports a mismatch, or reports \\toc1 or \\mt as missing if either is absent. No turns this comparison and its missing-marker checks off entirely.\par
\par
\b CompareToc2ToH\b0\par
\tab Default:  Yes\par
\tab Yes (default) compares \\toc2 against \\h (the running header text). Reports a mismatch, or reports \\toc2 or \\h as missing if either is absent. No turns this comparison and its missing-marker checks off entirely.\par
\par
\b CompareToc1ToBookNames\b0\par
\tab Default:  Yes\par
\tab Yes (default) compares \\toc1 against the "long" name for that book in the project's BookNames.xml file. Reports a mismatch, or reports \\toc1 as missing if absent. Silently skipped for a book if BookNames.xml is missing, unreadable, or has no long name on file for that book. No turns this comparison off entirely.\par
\par
\b CompareToc2ToBookNames\b0\par
\tab Default:  Yes\par
\tab Yes (default) compares \\toc2 against the "short" name for that book in the project's BookNames.xml file. Reports a mismatch, or reports \\toc2 as missing if absent. Silently skipped for a book if BookNames.xml is missing, unreadable, or has no short name on file for that book. No turns this comparison off entirely.\par
\par
\b IgnoreCapitalizationOnly\b0\par
\tab Default:  No\par
\tab Yes ignores (does not report) a mismatch between any two compared markers if the only difference is letter case -- the same test used for the "Capitalization only?" column in the HTML report. Spelling differences and substantively different text are still always reported. No (default) reports every mismatch, including pure capitalization differences, as today.\par
\par
\b IgnoreMt1\b0\par
\tab Default:  No\par
\tab Yes excludes \\mt1's text entirely when building the main title compared against \\toc1 -- useful for projects where \\mt1 holds something other than title wording (e.g. a short label) and the real title text is only in \\mt2/\\mt3. Does not affect the missing-\\mt check: if \\mt1 physically exists but is the only \\mt-level marker present, it is still not reported as missing, but there is then nothing left to compare against \\toc1, so no mismatch is reported either. No (default) includes \\mt1 as today.\par
\par
\b IgnoreMt2\b0\par
\tab Default:  No\par
\tab Yes excludes \\mt2's text entirely when building the main title compared against \\toc1 -- useful for projects where \\mt2 holds something other than title wording and the real title text is in \\mt1/\\mt3 instead. Does not affect the missing-\\mt check: if \\mt2 physically exists but is the only \\mt-level marker present, it is still not reported as missing, but there is then nothing left to compare against \\toc1, so no mismatch is reported either. No (default) includes \\mt2 as today.\par
\par
\b IgnoreMt3\b0\par
\tab Default:  No\par
\tab Yes excludes \\mt3's text entirely when building the main title compared against \\toc1 -- useful for projects where \\mt3 holds something other than title wording and the real title text is in \\mt1/\\mt2 instead. Does not affect the missing-\\mt check: if \\mt3 physically exists but is the only \\mt-level marker present, it is still not reported as missing, but there is then nothing left to compare against \\toc1, so no mismatch is reported either. No (default) includes \\mt3 as today.\par
\par
\b CheckNumberedBooks\b0\par
\tab Default:  Yes\par
\tab Yes (default) adds the numbered-books section described above to the HTML report. No leaves it out.\par
}
